  <!-- A compilation of stellar models replacing every source of yields in the standard compilation.

       Asymptotic giant branch (AGB) stars of 1-6 Msun (6.5 Msun at Z = 0.02) are from Karakas (2010), at
       Z = 0.0001, 0.004, 0.008 and 0.02. These extend a factor of forty below the Z = 0.004 floor of the Marigo
       (2001) models used by the standard compilation, and give yields for every element to nickel rather than for
       C, N and O alone.

       Super-AGB stars of 6.5-7.5 Msun are from Doherty et al. (2014a,b), at Z = 0.0001, 0.001, 0.004, 0.008 and
       0.02. These fill much of the gap between the AGB and massive star models, which in the standard compilation
       has no stellar models at all between 5 and 9 Msun. Doherty et al. models of 8 Msun and above (present at
       Z >= 0.004) are excluded from the converted file, since Galacticus treats stars above its
       initialMassForSupernovaeTypeII parameter (8 Msun by default) as supernova progenitors.

       Massive stars of 13-120 Msun are from Limongi & Chieffi (2018), explosion set M (mixing and fallback) for
       non-rotating models, as in stellarPropertiesCompilationLimongiChieffi2018.xml (which see for the alternative
       explosion sets and rotation velocities). Stars between the 8 Msun threshold and 13 Msun are assigned the
       yields per unit initial mass of the 13 Msun models. At Z < 0.01345 these models extend only to 80 Msun;
       above that (and above 120 Msun at Z = 0.01345) each metallicity's most massive model is used, scaled by
       initial mass.

       Pair instability supernovae are from Heger & Woosley (2002), at Z = 0 only.

       Lifetimes are from Portinari, Chiosi & Bressan (1998), with those of Tumlinson, Shull & Venkatesan (2003) at
       Z = 0 and those of Limongi & Chieffi (2018) at their metallicities. Neither Karakas (2010) nor Doherty et al.
       (2014) tabulate lifetimes.

       Limitations to be aware of when using this compilation:

       * Ejected masses between 8 and 13 Msun are not metallicity dependent. No metallicity in the compilation has
         both intermediate mass and massive star models (Karakas and Doherty et al. are at Z = 0.0001-0.02,
         Limongi & Chieffi at Z = 3.2e-5-0.01345). Below 8 Msun the ejected mass at each metallicity is taken from
         its most massive model (7.5 Msun), scaled by initial mass. Above 8 Msun the only tabulation reaching below
         13 Msun is that of Heger & Woosley (2002) at Z = 0, which is therefore used at all metallicities. Its
         values (6.8, 8.5 and 10.4 Msun at 8, 10 and 12 Msun) are close to the initial mass less that of a neutron
         star.

       * The metallicity scales of the sources differ (Karakas and Doherty et al. quote nominal Z, with Z = 0.02
         for their Solar models; Limongi & Chieffi quote the metal mass fraction of a scaled Asplund et al. 2009
         mixture, with Z = 0.01345 at Solar). The tabulated values are used as given.

       * Yields change abruptly at 6.5 Msun for 0.0001 < Z < 0.004. Karakas (2010) has no models at Z = 0.001,
         so there the compilation has only the Doherty et al. models of 6.5-7.5 Msun. Below 6.5 Msun, a star at
         such a metallicity is interpolated between Z = 0.0001 and Z = 0.004. From 6.5 Msun the Z = 0.001 models
         are used instead. The yields of these stars fall steeply between Z = 0.0001 and 0.001, so the straddling
         interpolation below 6.5 Msun overestimates them (by up to about 60% in the metal yield of a 6.5 Msun star
         at Z = 0.002). Integrated over the initial mass function the effect is below 0.2% in the metal yield and
         negligible for C, O and Fe, but reaches about 10% for N, which these stars mainly produce, near Z = 0.001.

       * The AGB and super-AGB models give no yield for any element beyond nickel. Their s-process production is
         tabulated only as a total (the species "g"), which is included in the metal yield but cannot be assigned
         to any element.

       * The minimum initial mass for a Type II supernova is independent of metallicity in Galacticus, while in the
         Doherty et al. models it rises with metallicity, to above 9 Msun at Z = 0.02. Their models of 8 Msun and
         above, which end as white dwarfs, are therefore excluded, and stars between 8 Msun and the true
         supernova threshold are treated as supernova progenitors.

       * The later AGB models of Karakas & Lugaro (2016) and Karakas et al. (2018), which extend to
         Z = 0.0028-0.03 and up to 8 Msun, are not included: no machine-readable version with ejected masses was
         found, and their metallicities interleave with those of Karakas (2010). -->
  <xi:include href="stellarPropertiesKarakas2010.xml"                         xpointer="xpointer(stars/star)" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude"/>
